cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference this analysis, please cite it as below."
title: "ZFS SLOG hole-vdev recovery: a case study, a reproducible OpenZFS hang, and the patches that repaired one pool"
abstract: >-
  A case study of recovering a 186 TB raw (140 TB allocated) OpenZFS pool that
  a failed mirrored SLOG left unimportable through two independent on-disk
  defects. The first was a ZIL header DVA referencing a removed log vdev that
  the pool had retained as a hole, and every writable import hung on it. The
  second was space-map corruption, which tripped a hard assertion in
  space_map_load_callback. Both defects were repaired on disk, and a completed
  scrub verified the result. Accompanying reproduction work reproduces an
  unkillable hang from scratch on stock OpenZFS when a failing log device is
  removed, in which a read issued from zil_parse() never completes and the
  thread waits forever in zio_wait(). For that removal case the non-completion
  traces to zio_suspend() under the default failmode=wait. For the incident's
  import path it remains an open question: synthesising the
  hole-plus-dangling-DVA state on zfs-2.4.3 did not hang. How the incident pool
  reached its precondition proved unreproducible by any natural route across
  ten hypotheses and four ZFS versions, and it is classified as an anomaly of
  the hardware failure.
authors:
  - alias: Unnamed0834
date-released: 2026-08-04
